1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
Okay, moving on to lung cancer, this is the most common cause of cancer mortality in the United States.

2
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:10,400
Remember that by incidence, the most common cancers are, number one, breast or prostate,

3
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:12,860
depending on whether we're dealing with a female or a male.

4
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:18,060
Number two is lung cancer, and number three is colorectal carcinoma.

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:24,580
However, lung cancer moves up to become the most common cause of cancer mortality or death in the United States.

6
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:27,720
The average age of presentation is about 60 years of age.

7
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:31,160
Among the risk factors, the first is cigarette smoke.

8
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,340
85% of lung cancer occurs in smokers.

9
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:37,780
Cigarette smoke contains over 60 different carcinogens.

10
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:43,440
Particularly mutagenic within cigarette smoke are polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons.

11
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:44,240
That's high yield.

12
00:00:44,860 --> 00:00:50,620
Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons are especially mutagenic, and also present in cigarette smoke is arsenic.

13
00:00:50,660 --> 00:00:56,400
And you'll recall from our neoplasia lecture that arsenic increases the risk for squamous cell carcinoma of the lung.

14
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:03,680
Of course, risk is related to the duration and the amount of smoking, and that can be summarized as the pack year measurement.

15
00:01:04,380 --> 00:01:12,100
And so we ask patients how many packs of cigarettes they smoke per day, and for how many years they've been smoking that number of packs.

16
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:15,520
So these are all the key features concerning cigarette smoke.

17
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:21,780
As far as radon is concerned, the way I think about radon is sort of the radon-uranium sequence.

18
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:29,000
Now, radon is generated from radioactive decay of uranium, and uranium is present in the soil all around us.

19
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,620
This normally goes up into the atmosphere and doesn't really cause any problems.

20
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:40,760
However, it can accumulate in closed spaces, such as basements, as a colorless, odorless gas called radon.

21
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:45,840
And of course, that would eventually go into the lung, increasing the risk of lung cancer.

22
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:55,040
Now, if you remember this radon-uranium sequence, it also helps you to remember that the risk of lung cancer is increased in uranium miners as well.

23
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:59,340
And of course, they would also be breathing in this type of gas.

24
00:02:00,440 --> 00:02:11,520
The third risk factor is asbestos, and the most important thing to state here is that patients who are exposed to asbestos are much more likely to develop lung cancer than they are to develop mesothelioma.

25
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:14,840
I've mentioned that a few times in prior lectures as well.

26
00:02:16,060 --> 00:02:19,660
Now, patients who present with lung cancer, they present with nonspecific symptoms.

27
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:27,460
For example, they may present with cough, weight loss, hemoptysis, post-obstructive pneumonia, etc.

28
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:32,800
Whatever it might be, especially in a smoker, when you have these nonspecific types of symptoms,

29
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:37,500
the next thing that's going to happen is that the patient's going to get some sort of imaging, usually a chest x-ray.

30
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,360
And a chest x-ray would classically reveal something called a coin lesion, or a solitary nodule.

31
00:02:43,860 --> 00:02:48,880
A coin lesion, again, is a solitary nodule that measures about anywhere from 2 to 5 centimeters.

32
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:54,020
And once a patient has a solitary nodule, examiners like to ask you, what's the next step?

33
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:59,900
So the next step after finding a solitary nodule is to go back and compare against a prior x-ray.

34
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:07,900
Often these patients will have had several x-rays in the past, and you can review those x-rays to see whether this solitary nodule or coin lesion has been stable.

35
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:16,100
If the coin lesion has been present over a long period of time and is unchanged, it's much more likely that this coin lesion represents something benign.

36
00:03:16,100 --> 00:03:27,680
However, if the coin lesion is new, or the coin lesion is growing, that would raise the concern and potentially prompt a biopsy in order to try to make a diagnosis of cancer.

37
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:30,740
Of course, a biopsy would be necessary for such a diagnosis.

38
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:36,440
Now, it is possible for patients to have benign lesions that could present as coin lesions.

39
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,300
Benign lesions are much more common in young patients.

40
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:46,080
In fact, a young patient with a coin lesion, let's say less than the age of 40, is much more likely to have a benign lesion than they are to have cancer.

41
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:50,180
And the two high-yield benign lesions to be aware of are, number one, granuloma.

42
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,100
So granulomas can actually present as a coin lesion.

43
00:03:53,540 --> 00:04:02,420
Classically, a granuloma would be due to TB or fungus, and especially in the Midwest, you would think about histoplasma as the cause of a coin lesion.

44
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,460
And again, that's particularly high-yield.

45
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:12,140
Another lesion that can present as a coin lesion that ends up being benign is a bronchial hematoma.

46
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:13,540
Now, a few important things.

47
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:15,100
First of all, what is a hematoma?

48
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:21,620
A hematoma is a benign mass that is comprised of tissue that normally belongs in that location.

49
00:04:21,860 --> 00:04:23,260
However, it's disorganized.

50
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:27,680
And so the bronchial hematoma actually contains lung tissue and cartilage.

51
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:28,540
That's high-yield.

52
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:31,060
Lung tissue plus cartilage.

53
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:34,040
And this lesion is often calcified on imaging.

54
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:38,420
And so this is another potential cause of a benign coin lesion.

55
00:04:39,800 --> 00:04:48,600
Now, if malignant cells are seen in biopsy, then the classic division of lung carcinoma is small cell carcinoma versus non-small cell carcinoma.

56
00:04:49,340 --> 00:04:53,880
This distinction is made by the pathologist by looking at the cells under the microscope.

57
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:59,860
There are some characteristic features of small cell carcinoma, classically with the cells being small.

58
00:05:00,300 --> 00:05:04,320
And if the cells are not small, then it would be called non-small cell carcinoma.

59
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:07,780
And we'll talk about that subtype in a little more detail in a moment.

60
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:10,640
Now, why is this initial distinction so important?

61
00:05:10,640 --> 00:05:15,860
It's important because small cell carcinoma is usually not amenable to surgical resection.

62
00:05:16,180 --> 00:05:17,660
Instead, it's treated with chemotherapy.

63
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:25,860
Whereas non-small cell carcinoma is actually treated up front with surgical resection and usually does not respond well to chemotherapy.

64
00:05:25,860 --> 00:05:37,820
If you're having trouble remembering this, one of my students once said, it's as if the cells of small cell carcinoma are so small that the surgeon can't see them, i.e. they're not amenable to surgical resection.

65
00:05:37,820 --> 00:05:44,160
As far as non-small cell carcinoma is concerned, that can be divided into several major subtypes.

66
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:54,120
So when you're looking at the slide, if you see glands or mucous production, these are the two key features of adenocarcinoma and are high yield to understand.

67
00:05:54,600 --> 00:06:00,200
If you see glands or mucous production, then we call that an adenocarcinoma.

68
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:07,900
Whereas if we see keratin pearls or intercellular bridges, we call that a squamous cell carcinoma.

69
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:17,920
If we don't see glands and we don't see mucin and we don't see keratin pearls and we don't see intercellular bridges, then we simply call that large cell carcinoma.

70
00:06:18,260 --> 00:06:23,100
There is another subtype of non-small cell carcinoma called a carcinoid tumor.

71
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:27,080
That's a little bit of a special subtype and we'll discuss that at the very end.

72
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:34,760
With that background in mind, let's review some of the major histologic subtypes of lung cancer present in this table in your text.

73
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:37,780
The first that I mention is small cell carcinoma.

74
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:42,740
Of course, we always start there because we want to divide small cell carcinoma from everything else.

75
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:50,820
And that's because, again, small cell carcinoma is treated up front with chemotherapy, as opposed to the others which would be treated up front with surgery.

76
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:56,900
Now, small cell carcinoma's characteristic histology will be poorly differentiated small cells.

77
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:03,900
Often, the tumor is mitotically active and exhibits necrosis and then has characteristic features of small cell carcinoma.

78
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:07,660
This is going to be highly associated with male smokers.

79
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:18,780
It will be a central tumor and it will have particular perineoplastic syndrome, such as antidiuretic hormone or ACTH production, giving the patient Cushing syndrome.

80
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:30,940
Or it can also be associated with a syndrome called Eaton-Lambert syndrome, in which patients develop antibodies against presynaptic calcium channels, resulting in the possibility of muscle weakness.

81
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:42,460
Now, let me just give you a little mnemonic here that some people feel is useful, and that is when the tumor starts with an S, in this case, small cell, then it's going to be highly associated with smoking.

82
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:49,000
It's going to be central, which starts with the S sound, and it will be associated with perineoplastic syndromes.

83
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:53,020
So, again, small cell, smokers, central syndromes.

84
00:07:53,320 --> 00:08:00,060
So, again, all of that starts with S, and that's one way by which people try to keep this information organized in their mind.

85
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:03,100
Here's a histologic image of small cell carcinoma.

86
00:08:03,440 --> 00:08:05,160
These are relatively small cells.

87
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:08,340
They sort of mimic lymphocytes in their size.

88
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:12,460
There's also a significant degree of mitotic activity.

89
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:16,700
And so, these are some of the features of small cell carcinoma.

90
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:22,640
Now, moving beyond small cell carcinoma, the next tumor to be aware of is squamous cell carcinoma.

91
00:08:23,080 --> 00:08:26,340
Of course, squamous cell carcinoma will be defined by one of two things.

92
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:32,220
We either have to see keratin, pearls, or intercellular bridges on the histology.

93
00:08:32,700 --> 00:08:34,020
Now, I'd like to make a note here.

94
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:36,540
Your book says intracellular.

95
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:41,200
It should read inter, I-N-T-E-R, cellular bridges.

96
00:08:41,200 --> 00:08:42,960
And so, please make that correction.

97
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:49,080
Again, squamous cell carcinoma is defined by the presence of keratin, pearls, or intercellular bridges.

98
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:53,420
So, we can use our mnemonic to remind ourselves that this is an S tumor.

99
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:54,580
It starts with the letter S.

100
00:08:54,860 --> 00:08:58,060
So, therefore, it's going to be highly associated with male smokers.

101
00:08:58,200 --> 00:09:00,960
In fact, it's the most common tumor in male smokers.

102
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:03,880
It'll be central, which starts with the S sound.

103
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:10,300
And it'll have an associated syndrome, which is the production of parathyroid hormone-related peptide,

104
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:12,260
giving the patients hypercalcemia.

105
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:16,460
Now, this is really only seen in a small subset of patients with squamous cell carcinoma,

106
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:21,280
but it's very classically seen in patients, especially on board exams.

107
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:28,120
So, you do need to be aware of the fact that squamous cell carcinoma can produce parathyroid hormone-related peptide.

108
00:09:28,120 --> 00:09:36,020
Moving to a couple images, this is a picture of a lung cancer, and we can clearly see that it's producing these pink keratin pearls.

109
00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:40,300
And so, hence, this would be an example of a squamous cell carcinoma.

110
00:09:40,740 --> 00:09:47,220
In addition, in this particular case, we can see that the tumor cells are exhibiting intercellular bridges.

111
00:09:47,220 --> 00:09:50,200
You can see these lines connecting the cells together.

112
00:09:50,340 --> 00:09:56,360
These are called intercellular bridges, and they represent the desmosomal connections between the squamous cells.

113
00:09:56,720 --> 00:09:59,440
Now, remember that squamous cells are normally connected by desmosomes,

114
00:09:59,820 --> 00:10:02,440
and so as the cells are pulled apart from one another,

115
00:10:02,920 --> 00:10:06,560
you can actually see the bridges of the connections between the desmosomes.

116
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:10,220
And, of course, desmosomes would indicate that we're dealing with squamous cells.

117
00:10:10,540 --> 00:10:14,660
So, hence, intracellular bridges are a sign of squamous cell carcinoma.

118
00:10:14,660 --> 00:10:17,380
Here's another example of a squamous cell carcinoma.

119
00:10:17,720 --> 00:10:21,760
Notice that this is a large airway, and this tumor is growing central.

120
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:27,420
And so, remember that squamous cell carcinoma, most common tumor in male smokers,

121
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:35,420
it is going to be central, and its paraneoplastic syndrome is going to be the production of parathyroid hormone-related peptide.

122
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:38,700
Now, the next type of tumor is adenocarcinoma.

123
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:41,840
This is defined by glands or mucin production.

124
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:45,320
So, either of these two features would define adenocarcinoma.

125
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:48,720
This is the most common tumor in non-smokers.

126
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:49,680
That's high yield.

127
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:53,680
And it's also the most common tumor in female smokers.

128
00:10:53,980 --> 00:10:58,360
Most common tumor in non-smokers and most common tumor in female smokers.

129
00:10:58,820 --> 00:11:02,080
This is classically located in a peripheral location,

130
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:05,880
and it's not associated with any paraneoplastic syndrome.

131
00:11:05,880 --> 00:11:09,060
Here's a classic image of an adenocarcinoma.

132
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:14,880
Here in the center, you can clearly see that the tumor cells are forming a glandular-like space.

133
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:20,780
Here as well, you can see that the tumor cells are forming this glandular-like space.

134
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:27,280
In addition, you can see that some of the tumor cells actually have this blue-gray mucus within the cytoplasm,

135
00:11:27,280 --> 00:11:32,500
which is called a mucin, and that is also indicative of an adenocarcinoma.

136
00:11:32,960 --> 00:11:35,040
Here's a classic image that can show up on an exam.

137
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:36,660
Notice that this is a tumor.

138
00:11:36,820 --> 00:11:39,240
You can clearly see the tumor here within the lung.

139
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:42,920
And notice that it's way out at the edge of the lung, up against the pleura.

140
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:48,480
And so, again, this is a peripheral location, and adenocarcinomas are classically peripheral.

141
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:51,980
Now, remember that if we have a non-small cell carcinoma,

142
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:57,060
and there are no keratin pearls, no intracellular bridges, no glands, or no mucin,

143
00:11:57,360 --> 00:11:59,680
then we call that large cell carcinoma.

144
00:11:59,980 --> 00:12:04,620
So this is comprised of poorly differentiated large cells with no keratin pearls,

145
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:07,800
no intracellular bridges, no glands, or no mucin.

146
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:09,900
It is associated with smoking.

147
00:12:10,180 --> 00:12:13,400
It can be central or peripheral, and it has a poor prognosis.

148
00:12:13,880 --> 00:12:16,920
So this is another subtype of lung cancer to be aware of.

149
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:20,220
Now, there are a few special subtypes that are particularly high-yield,

150
00:12:20,300 --> 00:12:21,640
so I'd like to touch on each of them.

151
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:25,620
The first is called bronchiolo-alveolar carcinoma.

152
00:12:26,440 --> 00:12:29,500
Bronchiolo means that we're dealing with the small airways,

153
00:12:29,840 --> 00:12:32,240
and alveolar means that we're dealing with the air sacs.

154
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:39,760
And this is a tumor that just grows along pre-existing small airways, bronchioles, and alveoli.

155
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:43,760
It arises from a cell called the Clara cell in the lung.

156
00:12:44,120 --> 00:12:45,440
It's not related to smoking.

157
00:12:45,440 --> 00:12:49,520
It's classically peripheral, and one of the very important features to be aware of

158
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:53,940
is that this tumor can present with pneumonia-like consolidation on imaging,

159
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:56,320
but it has an excellent prognosis.

160
00:12:56,920 --> 00:13:01,620
And so, again, these are a few classic features of bronchiolo-alveolar carcinoma.

161
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:03,880
Here's a picture of this type of tumor.

162
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:07,740
Here, you can see that this is the normal alveolar air sac.

163
00:13:07,740 --> 00:13:11,160
And here, you can see that this wall of the alveolar air sac,

164
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:13,940
here and throughout the rest of the image here,

165
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:18,420
the walls of the alveolar air sac are all replaced by these tall columnar cells.

166
00:13:18,840 --> 00:13:22,840
And so, this basically represents bronchiolo-alveolar carcinoma.

167
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:28,480
Again, classically presents with pneumonia-like consolidation on imaging,

168
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:31,340
and has an excellent prognosis.

169
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:35,920
Another special subtype of lung cancer to be aware of is called a carcinoid tumor.

170
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:40,120
This is a tumor of well-differentiated neuroendocrine cells.

171
00:13:40,460 --> 00:13:45,000
Very important for purposes of board examination, it is chromogranin positive.

172
00:13:46,020 --> 00:13:47,600
Again, it's a neuroendocrine cell,

173
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:52,080
and neuroendocrine cells contain neurosecretory granules within the cytoplasm.

174
00:13:52,540 --> 00:13:57,540
Those neurosecretory granules on immunohistochemistry will stain chromogranin positive.

175
00:13:57,540 --> 00:14:04,160
And board examiners like you to know that there will be chromogranin positivity in neuroendocrine tumors.

176
00:14:04,660 --> 00:14:09,720
So, just as a reminder, this is the second type of neuroendocrine tumor that we've talked about.

177
00:14:10,060 --> 00:14:14,980
Remember that small cell carcinoma was a poorly differentiated neuroendocrine tumor,

178
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:19,700
and that carcinoid tumor is a well-differentiated neuroendocrine tumor.

179
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:22,920
And both would be chromogranin positive.

180
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:26,040
Now, carcinoid tumors are not related to smoking.

181
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:32,660
They can be central or peripheral, but the classic tumor forms a polyp-like mass in the bronchus,

182
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:35,180
and that's the way it shows up on examinations as well.

183
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:40,400
This is a low-grade malignancy, and it rarely can cause carcinoid syndrome.

184
00:14:40,560 --> 00:14:41,780
Now, usually it doesn't.

185
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,840
However, on examinations it can.

186
00:14:44,340 --> 00:14:46,780
And so, it rarely can cause carcinoid syndrome.

187
00:14:47,520 --> 00:14:49,160
Here's a picture of a carcinoid tumor.

188
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:54,060
This is the gross picture showing you a polyp-like mass in the bronchus.

189
00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:56,700
Here's a picture of the tumor on histology.

190
00:14:57,120 --> 00:15:02,040
You have nests of cells, which is a characteristic growth pattern of neuroendocrine cells.

191
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:04,740
And you can see that these cells are not mitotically active.

192
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:05,900
There is no necrosis.

193
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:09,900
And each group of tumor cells is sort of growing in these nests,

194
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:12,220
and that's characteristic of a carcinoid tumor.

195
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:16,080
Here is the chromogranin positivity that shows up on board examinations.

196
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:20,340
Now, remember that carcinoid tumors are neuroendocrine-type tumors,

197
00:15:20,840 --> 00:15:25,440
and hence they will stay positive for chromogranin by immunohistochemistry.

198
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:29,300
Last point that I would like to make concerning the types of lung cancer

199
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:31,680
is that we can also have metastasis to the lung.

200
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:35,740
The most common sources are breast and colon carcinoma.

201
00:15:36,420 --> 00:15:38,480
When patients have metastasis to the lung,

202
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:42,160
it usually presents as multiple cannonball nodules on imaging,

203
00:15:42,160 --> 00:15:46,020
so that you have multiple circular nodules within the lung,

204
00:15:46,440 --> 00:15:49,760
and metastasis is more common than primary tumors.

205
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:54,560
And so the lung is a common site for metastatic disease.

206
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:57,680
Now, when patients have primary carcinoma of the lung,

207
00:15:57,840 --> 00:15:59,960
we have to perform TNM staging.

208
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:03,520
The T is based on the size and local extension of the tumor.

209
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:07,220
The N is based on spread to the regional lymph nodes,

210
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,500
in particular the hylar and mediastinal nodes,

211
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:11,420
and the M is based on metastasis.

212
00:16:11,420 --> 00:16:14,520
One very high-yield point to be aware of,

213
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:16,180
especially for board examination,

214
00:16:16,640 --> 00:16:20,800
is that the adrenal gland is a unique site for lung metastasis.

215
00:16:21,340 --> 00:16:24,460
The lung likes to metastasize to the adrenal gland.

216
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:27,900
The overall five-year survival for lung cancer is fairly poor,

217
00:16:28,300 --> 00:16:31,020
and that's because it presents so late in the course.

218
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:33,920
Any cancer that presents late in the course

219
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:37,420
will often have a very poor survival.

220
00:16:37,420 --> 00:16:41,460
And, of course, we do not have effective screening methods for lung cancer,

221
00:16:41,560 --> 00:16:44,180
which is one reason why it presents so late in the course.

222
00:16:44,840 --> 00:16:49,060
Now, local complications are related to local extension of the tumor.

223
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:52,400
So, for example, we can see pleural involvement.

224
00:16:52,400 --> 00:16:55,000
Which tumor would classically involve the pleura?

225
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,420
The answer would be adenocarcinoma,

226
00:16:57,720 --> 00:17:00,100
and that's because adenocarcinoma is usually peripheral.

227
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:04,720
So, remember that adenocarcinoma is the most common tumor seen in non-smokers

228
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:06,640
and female smokers,

229
00:17:06,700 --> 00:17:07,820
and it's usually peripheral,

230
00:17:08,260 --> 00:17:09,620
hence the pleura could be involved.

231
00:17:10,140 --> 00:17:12,260
We can see obstruction of the superior vena cava.

232
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:13,600
When that occurs,

233
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:16,460
we get something called the superior vena cava syndrome.

234
00:17:16,460 --> 00:17:19,600
This is where the patients have distended head and neck veins

235
00:17:19,600 --> 00:17:22,800
with edema and blue discoloration of the arms and face.

236
00:17:23,480 --> 00:17:25,120
Again, not too commonly seen,

237
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:26,820
except on board examinations.

238
00:17:27,560 --> 00:17:30,300
You can also get involvement of the recurrent laryngeal nerve,

239
00:17:30,540 --> 00:17:31,800
which would create hoarseness,

240
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:33,240
or the phrenic nerve,

241
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:35,580
which would create diaphragmatic paralysis.

242
00:17:36,940 --> 00:17:39,880
And finally, the tumor can compress the sympathetic chain,

243
00:17:40,260 --> 00:17:43,120
particularly when it's located in the apex of the lung.

244
00:17:43,120 --> 00:17:45,360
That's also called a pancos tumor,

245
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:47,800
i.e. a tumor located in the apex of the lung

246
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:49,300
is also called a pancos tumor.

247
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:51,420
And when you compress the sympathetic chain,

248
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:53,900
we get ptosis, which is drooping of the eyelid.

249
00:17:54,280 --> 00:17:55,680
We get pinpoint pupil,

250
00:17:56,440 --> 00:17:57,460
and anhidrosis,

251
00:17:57,540 --> 00:17:58,640
which would be lack of sweating,

252
00:17:58,740 --> 00:17:59,900
particularly of the forehead.

253
00:18:00,460 --> 00:18:02,680
And that concludes our discussion of lung cancer.

